WEBVTT

1
00:00:55.796 --> 00:00:58.993
<i>There are some fish</i>
<i>that cannot be caught.</i>

2
00:01:01.068 --> 00:01:04.162
<i>It's not that they're faster</i>
<i>or stronger than other fish.</i>

3
00:01:04.338 --> 00:01:07.501
<i>They're just touched</i>
<i>by something extra.</i>

4
00:01:08.776 --> 00:01:11.438
<i>One such fish was The Beast.</i>

5
00:01:13.114 --> 00:01:17.175
<i>And by the time I was born</i>
<i>he was already a legend.</i>

6
00:01:17.351 --> 00:01:21.947
<i>He'd passed up more $100 lures</i>
<i>than any fish in Alabama.</i>

7
00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:26.116
<i>Some said that fish was</i>
<i>the ghost of a thief...</i>

8
00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:29.491
<i>...who'd drowned in that river</i>
<i>60 years before.</i>

9
00:01:30.998 --> 00:01:35.697
Others claimed he was a dinosaur
left over from the "Cruaceous" Period.

10
00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.704
I didn't put any stock into
such speculation or superstition.

11
00:01:39.874 --> 00:01:42.570
All I knew was I'd been trying
to catch that fish...

12
00:01:42.743 --> 00:01:45.234
...since I was a boy
no bigger than you.

13
00:01:45.813 --> 00:01:48.247
And on the day you were born...

14
00:01:48.416 --> 00:01:51.385
Well, that was the day
I finally caught him.

15
00:01:53.287 --> 00:01:55.084
Now, I'd tried everything on it:

16
00:01:55.256 --> 00:01:59.090
Worms, lures, peanut butter,
peanut butter and cheese.

17
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.854
But on that day I had a revelation:

18
00:02:02.029 --> 00:02:06.864
If that fish was Henry Walls' ghost,
then the usual bait wasn't gonna work.

19
00:02:07.034 --> 00:02:11.334
I was gonna have to use something
he truly desired.

20
00:02:12.273 --> 00:02:14.901
-Your finger?
-Gold.

21
00:02:15.743 --> 00:02:18.974
Now, I tied my ring onto
the strongest line they made...

22
00:02:19.146 --> 00:02:23.378
...strong enough to hold up a bridge,
they said, if only for a few minutes.

23
00:02:24.452 --> 00:02:27.250
And then I cast upriver.

24
00:02:30.024 --> 00:02:34.654
The Beast jumped up and grabbed it
before it even hit the water.

25
00:02:34.829 --> 00:02:39.266
And just as fast,
he snapped clean through that line.

26
00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:41.128
Well, you can see my predicament.

27
00:02:41.302 --> 00:02:45.295
My wedding ring,
the symbol of fidelity to my wife...

28
00:02:45.539 --> 00:02:48.201
-...soon to be mother of my child...
-Make him stop.

29
00:02:48.376 --> 00:02:51.277
...was now lost in the gut
of an uncatchable fish.

30
00:02:51.645 --> 00:02:53.112
What did you do?

31
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.579
I followed that fish upriver
and downriver.

32
00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:00.719
This fish, The Beast...

33
00:03:00.888 --> 00:03:06.849
...the whole time we were calling it
a him, when in fact it was a her.

34
00:03:07.027 --> 00:03:09.325
It was fat with eggs
it was gonna lay any day.

35
00:03:09.497 --> 00:03:12.159
Now, I was in a situation.

36
00:03:12.333 --> 00:03:15.962
I could gut that fish
and get my wedding ring back...

37
00:03:16.604 --> 00:03:21.405
...but in doing so I'd be killing the
smartest catfish in the Ashton River.

38
00:03:21.575 --> 00:03:24.009
Did I want to deprive
my son the chance...

39
00:03:24.178 --> 00:03:27.204
...to catch a fish like this
of his own?

40
00:03:27.381 --> 00:03:30.009
This ladyfish and I...

41
00:03:30.184 --> 00:03:34.678
-Well, we had the same destiny.
-"We were part of the same equation."

42
00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.883
Now, you may well ask...

43
00:03:38.058 --> 00:03:41.789
Oh, darling, darling,
it's still your night.

44
00:03:41.962 --> 00:03:47.832
...why did it strike so quick on gold
when nothing else would attract it?

45
00:03:48.002 --> 00:03:51.130
That was the lesson
I learned that day...

46
00:03:51.305 --> 00:03:54.331
...the day my son was born.

47
00:03:54.508 --> 00:04:00.572
Sometimes the only way to catch
an uncatchable woman...

48
00:04:00.748 --> 00:04:04.275
...is to offer her a wedding ring.

49
00:04:12.693 --> 00:04:15.389
What, a father's not allowed
to talk about his son?

50
00:04:15.563 --> 00:04:17.394
I'm a footnote in that story, Dad...

51
00:04:17.565 --> 00:04:23.526
...the context for your great adventure,
which never happened, incidentally.

52
00:04:23.704 --> 00:04:26.867
You were selling novelty products
in Wichita when I was born.

53
00:04:27.041 --> 00:04:29.635
Come on, Will.
Everyone loves that story!

54
00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:34.179
They don't. I don't love that story. Not
anymore. Not after a thousand times!

55
00:04:34.348 --> 00:04:38.284
I know every punch line, Dad.
I can tell them as well as you can!

56
00:04:38.452 --> 00:04:41.751
For one night,
one night in your entire life...

57
00:04:41.922 --> 00:04:45.050
...the universe did not revolve
around Edward Bloom.

58
00:04:45.226 --> 00:04:49.492
-How can you not understand that?
-I'm sorry to embarrass you.

59
00:04:49.663 --> 00:04:53.565
You're embarrassing yourself, Dad.
You just don't see it.

60
00:04:55.703 --> 00:04:59.764
<i>After that night, I didn't speak</i>
<i>to my father again for three years.</i>

61
00:05:05.279 --> 00:05:09.272
William Bloom, United Press
International. If I could just...

62
00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.580
<i>We communicated indirectly, I guess.</i>

63
00:05:15.756 --> 00:05:19.783
<i>In her letters and Christmas cards,</i>
<i>my mother wrote for both of them.</i>

64
00:05:20.094 --> 00:05:25.031
<i>And when I'd call, she'd say Dad was</i>
<i>out driving or swimming in the pool.</i>

65
00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:28.225
<i>True to form, we never</i>
<i>talked about not talking.</i>

66
00:05:30.571 --> 00:05:34.940
<i>The truth is, I didn't see anything</i>
<i>of myself in my father.</i>

67
00:05:35.109 --> 00:05:38.442
<i>And I don't think he saw anything</i>
<i>of himself in me.</i>

68
00:05:41.982 --> 00:05:45.577
<i>We were like strangers</i>
<i>who knew each other very well.</i>

69
00:05:50.724 --> 00:05:53.056
<i>In telling the story</i>
<i>of my father's life...</i>

70
00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:57.721
<i>...it's impossible to separate fact</i>
<i>from fiction, the man from the myth.</i>

71
00:06:00.534 --> 00:06:04.368
<i>The best I can do</i>
<i>is to tell it the way he told me.</i>

72
00:06:06.106 --> 00:06:09.564
<i>It doesn't always make sense,</i>
<i>and most of it never happened.</i>

73
00:06:34.635 --> 00:06:37.297
Give me back my ring!

74
00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.208
Thank you!

75
00:06:51.518 --> 00:06:54.749
<i>But that's what kind of story this is.</i>

76
00:07:03.797 --> 00:07:07.062
<i>His birth would set the pace</i>
<i>for his unlikely life.</i>

77
00:07:07.234 --> 00:07:10.260
<i>No longer than most men's,</i>
<i>but larger.</i>

78
00:07:10.437 --> 00:07:12.803
<i>And as strange as his stories got...</i>

79
00:07:12.973 --> 00:07:16.465
<i>...the endings were always</i>
<i>the most surprising of all.</i>

80
00:07:43.203 --> 00:07:45.967
Yes. Yes, he's here.

81
00:07:47.074 --> 00:07:48.871
It's your mother.

82
00:07:55.849 --> 00:07:57.714
And what does Dr. Bennett say?

83
00:07:59.687 --> 00:08:01.917
No, sure, I'll talk to him.

84
00:08:02.956 --> 00:08:04.947
Yeah, I'll wait.

85
00:08:05.125 --> 00:08:06.592
It's bad?

86
00:08:06.794 --> 00:08:10.890
Yeah, it's more than they thought.
They're gonna stop chemo.

87
00:08:12.099 --> 00:08:14.795
-You need to go.
-Probably tonight.

88
00:08:15.703 --> 00:08:19.195
-I'm going with you.
-No, no, no. You shouldn't.

89
00:08:19.373 --> 00:08:21.170
I'm going with you.

90
00:08:50.471 --> 00:08:52.302
Now, which one's it gonna be?

91
00:08:52.473 --> 00:08:55.340
"Monkey in a Barn,"
or "Dog in the Road"?

92
00:08:55.442 --> 00:08:56.110
The one about the witch.

93
00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:57.111
The one about the witch.

94
00:08:57.111 --> 00:08:57.611
The one about the witch.

95
00:08:58.112 --> 00:08:59.613
Your momma says I can't
tell you that one anymore.

96
00:08:59.613 --> 00:09:00.447
Your momma says I can't
tell you that one anymore.

97
00:09:01.115 --> 00:09:04.710
-You get nightmares.
-But I'm not scared.

98
00:09:06.487 --> 00:09:09.012
Well, neither was I, at first.

99
00:09:09.523 --> 00:09:12.424
Now, this took place
in the swamp outside of Ashton.

100
00:09:12.593 --> 00:09:17.257
Children weren't allowed in the swamp
on account of the snakes, spiders...

101
00:09:17.431 --> 00:09:22.061
...and quicksand that would swallow
you up before you could even scream.

102
00:09:22.236 --> 00:09:24.466
There were five of us
out there that night:

103
00:09:25.105 --> 00:09:28.472
<i>Me, Ruthie, Wilbur Freely...</i>

104
00:09:28.642 --> 00:09:32.305
<i>...and the Price Brothers,</i>
<i>Don and Zacky.</i>

105
00:09:32.479 --> 00:09:36.313
<i>And not one of us knew</i>
<i>what was in store.</i>

106
00:09:36.884 --> 00:09:41.082
<i>It's common knowledge that most</i>
<i>towns of a certain size have a witch...</i>

107
00:09:41.255 --> 00:09:43.450
<i>...if only to eat</i>
<i>misbehaving children...</i>

108
00:09:43.624 --> 00:09:46.821
<i>...and the occasional puppy</i>
<i>who wanders into her yard.</i>

109
00:09:46.994 --> 00:09:50.157
<i>Witches use those bones</i>
<i>to cast spells and curses...</i>

110
00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.730
<i>...and make the land infertile.</i>

111
00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:57.030
-Is it true she got a glass eye?
-I heard she got it from gypsies.

112
00:09:57.204 --> 00:09:59.399
-What's a gypsy?
-Your momma's a gypsy.

113
00:09:59.573 --> 00:10:00.870
Your momma's a bitch.

114
00:10:01.041 --> 00:10:03.271
You shouldn't swear.
There's ladies present.

115
00:10:03.443 --> 00:10:04.671
-Shit.
-Damn.

116
00:10:04.845 --> 00:10:05.903
Screw.

117
00:10:06.079 --> 00:10:08.172
Turn off your flashlights.
She'll see you!

118
00:10:08.348 --> 00:10:10.976
<i>Yet of all the witches in Alabama...</i>

119
00:10:11.151 --> 00:10:14.052
<i>...there was one who was said</i>
<i>to be the most feared.</i>

120
00:10:14.221 --> 00:10:18.317
<i>For she had one glass eye which</i>
<i>was said to contain mystical powers.</i>

121
00:10:18.492 --> 00:10:22.360
I heard if you look right at it,
you'll see how you're gonna die.

122
00:10:22.529 --> 00:10:26.226
That's bull-S-H-l-T, that is.
She's not even a real witch.

123
00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:28.459
If you're so sure, go get that eye.

124
00:10:28.635 --> 00:10:33.231
I heard she keeps it in a box on her
night table. Or are you too scared?

125
00:10:33.407 --> 00:10:35.602
I'll go in right now and get that eye.

126
00:10:35.776 --> 00:10:37.835
-Then do it.
-Fine, I will.

127
00:10:38.011 --> 00:10:40.980
-Fine, you do it.
-Fine, I'm doing it.

128
00:10:41.148 --> 00:10:43.412
Edward, don't!

129
00:10:43.584 --> 00:10:47.520
She'll make soap out of you.
That's what she does.

130
00:10:47.688 --> 00:10:50.350
She makes soap out of people.

131
00:11:40.173 --> 00:11:42.505
Ma'am, my name is Edward Bloom...

132
00:11:42.676 --> 00:11:45.304
...and there's some folks
like to see your eye.

133
00:11:58.492 --> 00:12:00.460
-You get The Eye?
-I brought it.

134
00:12:00.627 --> 00:12:01.753
Let's see it.

135
00:12:07.401 --> 00:12:09.301
Whoa, help!

136
00:12:19.179 --> 00:12:23.445
I saw how I was gonna die.
I was old and I fell.

137
00:12:24.184 --> 00:12:26.618
I wasn't old at all.

138
00:12:33.961 --> 00:12:37.692
I was thinking about death and all.
About seeing how you're gonna die.

139
00:12:37.864 --> 00:12:41.356
I mean, on one hand,
if dying was all you thought about...

140
00:12:41.668 --> 00:12:43.397
...it could kind of screw you up.

141
00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:47.301
But it could kind of help you,
couldn't it?

142
00:12:47.474 --> 00:12:51.570
Because, everything else,
you'd know you could survive.

143
00:12:54.381 --> 00:12:57.475
I guess I'm saying I'd like to know.

144
00:13:09.129 --> 00:13:10.960
That's how I go.

145
00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:34.680
Hi, Mom.

146
00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:45.828
-Is that Dr. Bennett's car?
-Yes. He's up with your father now.

147
00:13:46.199 --> 00:13:49.691
-And how is he?
-Well... No, I got it.

148
00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:53.931
He's impossible. He won't eat.
Because he doesn't eat, he's weaker.

149
00:13:54.107 --> 00:13:56.200
Because he's weak,
he doesn't want to eat.

150
00:13:56.376 --> 00:14:01.245
-How much time does he have left?
-You don't talk about that.

151
00:14:01.581 --> 00:14:03.105
Not yet.

152
00:14:04.785 --> 00:14:06.309
Will.

153
00:14:07.387 --> 00:14:10.379
Dr. Bennett. Oh, it's good to see you.

154
00:14:10.557 --> 00:14:13.117
My wife, Josephine.

155
00:14:13.927 --> 00:14:15.326
A pleasure.

156
00:14:16.029 --> 00:14:19.692
-You're seven months.
-To the day!

157
00:14:21.701 --> 00:14:22.963
It's a boy.

158
00:14:25.705 --> 00:14:31.302
Try to get him to drink one of these.
He won't, but go ahead and try.

159
00:15:17.224 --> 00:15:18.486
Dad?

160
00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:32.100
You want some water?

161
00:15:48.788 --> 00:15:52.747
-You are in for a surprise.
-Am I?

162
00:15:53.026 --> 00:15:55.859
Having a kid changes everything.

163
00:15:56.029 --> 00:15:59.795
There's the diapers and the burping
and the midnight feeding.

164
00:15:59.966 --> 00:16:02.958
-Did you do any of that?
-No.

165
00:16:03.603 --> 00:16:05.594
But I hear it's terrible.

166
00:16:06.106 --> 00:16:11.203
Then you spend years trying
to corrupt and mislead this child...

167
00:16:11.378 --> 00:16:16.179
...fill its head with nonsense,
and still it turns out perfectly fine.

168
00:16:16.683 --> 00:16:21.313
-You think I'm up for it?
-You learned from the best.

169
00:16:25.192 --> 00:16:28.093
Drink half the can.
I'll tell Mom you drank it all.

170
00:16:28.261 --> 00:16:29.819
Everyone wins.

171
00:16:30.363 --> 00:16:34.265
People needn't worry so much.
It's not my time yet.

172
00:16:34.434 --> 00:16:36.402
This is not how I go.

173
00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:40.768
-Really?
-Truly. I saw it in The Eye.

174
00:16:41.374 --> 00:16:44.741
-The old lady by the swamp?
-She was a witch.

175
00:16:44.911 --> 00:16:47.573
No, she was old and probably senile.

176
00:16:47.747 --> 00:16:52.912
I saw my death in that eye,
and this isn't how it happens.

177
00:16:53.086 --> 00:16:55.145
So how does it happen?

178
00:16:55.322 --> 00:16:59.884
Surprise ending.
Wouldn't want to ruin it for you.

179
00:17:01.061 --> 00:17:03.928
Your mother thought
we wouldn't talk again.

180
00:17:04.097 --> 00:17:06.895
Look at us. We're talking fine.

181
00:17:07.067 --> 00:17:08.932
We're storytellers, both of us.

182
00:17:09.102 --> 00:17:14.597
I speak mine out,
you write yours down. Same thing.

183
00:17:16.042 --> 00:17:17.703
Dad...

184
00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:23.108
I hope to talk about some things
while I'm here.

185
00:17:23.283 --> 00:17:25.478
You mean while I'm here.

186
00:17:25.952 --> 00:17:29.683
I just want to know
the true versions of things:

187
00:17:29.856 --> 00:17:32.654
Events. Stories.

188
00:17:32.959 --> 00:17:34.187
You.

189
00:17:45.905 --> 00:17:48.499
Your mother hasn't been
keeping up the pool.

190
00:17:48.675 --> 00:17:52.338
-If you wanted to, you could fix it.
-Yeah, I will.

191
00:17:52.512 --> 00:17:55.777
-You know where the chemicals are?
-I did it when you were gone.

192
00:17:55.949 --> 00:18:01.945
I was never much for being
at home, Will. Too confining.

193
00:18:02.489 --> 00:18:05.185
And this here, being stuck in bed...

194
00:18:05.392 --> 00:18:08.884
Dying is the worst thing
that's ever happened to me.

195
00:18:09.829 --> 00:18:15.495
-I thought you said you weren't dying.
-I said this isn't how I go.

196
00:18:15.669 --> 00:18:19.571
The last part is much more unusual.

197
00:18:19.739 --> 00:18:22.071
Trust me on that.

198
00:18:54.974 --> 00:18:57.943
Dr. Bennett said I have
to stay at home for a week.

199
00:18:58.111 --> 00:19:01.808
Oh, that's nothing. Once,
I had to stay in bed for three years.

200
00:19:01.981 --> 00:19:05.212
-Did you have the chickenpox?
-I wish.

201
00:19:24.738 --> 00:19:27.434
<i>Truth is, no one quite knew</i>
<i>what was wrong.</i>

202
00:19:27.774 --> 00:19:30.265
<i>Most times a person</i>
<i>grows up gradually...</i>

203
00:19:30.443 --> 00:19:32.809
<i>...while I found myself in a hurry.</i>

204
00:19:36.049 --> 00:19:40.213
<i>My muscles and my bones couldn't</i>
<i>keep up with my body's ambition.</i>

205
00:19:40.387 --> 00:19:43.845
<i>So I spent the better part</i>
<i>of three years confined to my bed...</i>

206
00:19:44.023 --> 00:19:47.959
<i>...with the encyclopedia being</i>
<i>my only means of exploration.</i>

207
00:19:48.128 --> 00:19:50.119
<i>I had made it all the way to the G's...</i>

208
00:19:50.296 --> 00:19:53.197
<i>...hoping to find an answer</i>
<i>to my "gigantificationism"...</i>

209
00:19:53.366 --> 00:19:57.200
<i>...when I uncovered an article</i>
<i>about the common goldfish.</i>

210
00:19:57.404 --> 00:20:01.306
"Kept in a small bowl,
the goldfish will remain small.

211
00:20:01.474 --> 00:20:02.805
With more space...

212
00:20:02.976 --> 00:20:06.810
...the fish will grow double, triple,
or quadruple its size."

213
00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:10.245
<i>It occurred to me then that perhaps</i>
<i>the reason for my growth...</i>

214
00:20:10.417 --> 00:20:12.942
<i>...was that I was intended</i>
<i>for larger things.</i>

215
00:20:13.119 --> 00:20:16.680
<i>After all, a giant man can't have</i>
<i>an ordinary-sized life.</i>

216
00:20:18.224 --> 00:20:21.489
<i>As soon as my bones had settled</i>
<i>in their adult configuration...</i>

217
00:20:21.661 --> 00:20:26.462
<i>...I set upon my plan to make</i>
<i>a bigger place for myself in Ashton.</i>

218
00:20:26.633 --> 00:20:27.895
<i>Tigers, go!</i>

219
00:20:43.383 --> 00:20:45.112
Edward Bloom!

220
00:21:27.927 --> 00:21:31.988
Doggy! My doggy!
My doggy's trapped!

221
00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:44.237
<i>I was the biggest thing</i>
<i>Ashton had ever seen.</i>

222
00:21:45.044 --> 00:21:48.036
<i>Until one day, a stranger arrived.</i>

223
00:22:03.296 --> 00:22:07.926
Calm down. Calm down, everybody.
Calm down. That's enough.

224
00:22:08.101 --> 00:22:11.867
-Mr. Mayor, he ate an entire corn field.
-He ate my dog.

225
00:22:12.038 --> 00:22:14.632
If you ain't gonna stop him, mayor,
we will.

226
00:22:14.807 --> 00:22:17.867
I won't have mob violence
in this town.

227
00:22:18.044 --> 00:22:19.978
Now, has someone tried
talking to him?

228
00:22:20.146 --> 00:22:22.774
-You can't reason with him.
-He's a monster.

229
00:22:23.049 --> 00:22:24.744
I'll do it.

230
00:22:39.432 --> 00:22:41.764
I'll talk to him.
See if I can get him to go.

231
00:22:41.935 --> 00:22:44.335
That creature could crush you
without trying.

232
00:22:44.504 --> 00:22:47.496
Oh, trust me, he'll have to try.

233
00:23:19.005 --> 00:23:20.495
Hello?

234
00:23:24.577 --> 00:23:28.104
My name is Edward Bloom,
and I wanna talk to you!

235
00:23:28.915 --> 00:23:31.281
Go away!

236
00:23:33.186 --> 00:23:36.246
Now, I'm not going anywhere
until you show yourself!

237
00:23:36.422 --> 00:23:39.414
I said, go away!

238
00:23:59.812 --> 00:24:02.246
<i>Armed with the foreknowledge</i>
<i>of my own death...</i>

239
00:24:02.415 --> 00:24:04.542
<i>...I knew the giant couldn't kill me.</i>

240
00:24:06.853 --> 00:24:10.152
<i>All the same, I preferred to keep</i>
<i>my bones unbroken.</i>

241
00:24:10.323 --> 00:24:12.314
Why are you here?

242
00:24:13.626 --> 00:24:15.116
So you can eat me.

243
00:24:15.595 --> 00:24:19.531
The town decided to send
a human sacrifice and I volunteered.

244
00:24:19.699 --> 00:24:23.294
My arms are a little stringy, but
there's some good eating in my legs.

245
00:24:23.469 --> 00:24:26.700
I mean, I'd be tempted
to eat them myself.

246
00:24:28.841 --> 00:24:30.934
So I guess, well...

247
00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:35.376
If you'd just get it over with quick,
because I'm not much for pain, really.

248
00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:51.557
Oh, come on! I can't go back!
I'm a human sacrifice!

249
00:24:51.731 --> 00:24:54.256
If I go back,
they'll think I'm a coward.

250
00:24:54.434 --> 00:24:57.028
I'd rather be dinner than a coward.

251
00:24:57.637 --> 00:24:58.865
Here.

252
00:24:59.372 --> 00:25:02.466
You can start with my hand.
It'll be an appetizer.

253
00:25:02.642 --> 00:25:06.772
I don't want to eat you.
I don't want to eat anybody.

254
00:25:06.946 --> 00:25:11.076
I just get so hungry.
I'm just too big.

255
00:25:12.251 --> 00:25:15.049
Did you ever think that
maybe you're not too big...

256
00:25:15.221 --> 00:25:17.348
...but maybe this town
is just too small?

257
00:25:17.523 --> 00:25:20.390
I've heard in real cities
there are buildings so tall...

258
00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:22.790
...you can't even see
the tops of them.

259
00:25:22.962 --> 00:25:25.453
-Really?
-Oh, I wouldn't lie to you.

260
00:25:25.631 --> 00:25:27.758
And all-you-can-eat buffets.

261
00:25:27.934 --> 00:25:30.926
-Now, you can eat a lot, can't you?
-I can.

262
00:25:31.104 --> 00:25:34.005
So why are you wasting your time
in a small town?

263
00:25:34.173 --> 00:25:37.267
You're a big man.
You should be in a big city.

264
00:25:37.443 --> 00:25:40.640
You're just trying to get me
to leave, aren't you?

265
00:25:40.947 --> 00:25:44.474
-What's your name, giant?
-Karl.

266
00:25:44.650 --> 00:25:48.086
Well, mine's Edward.
And truthfully...

267
00:25:48.254 --> 00:25:52.088
Well, I do want you to leave, Karl.
But I want to leave with you.

268
00:25:52.258 --> 00:25:55.022
I mean, you think this town
is too small for you?

269
00:25:55.194 --> 00:25:58.288
Well, it's too small for a man
of my ambition.

270
00:25:58.464 --> 00:26:01.627
So, what do you say? Join me?

271
00:26:04.837 --> 00:26:06.065
Okay.

272
00:26:07.406 --> 00:26:08.896
Okay.

273
00:26:09.175 --> 00:26:12.372
Now, first, we gotta
get you ready for the city.

274
00:26:29.128 --> 00:26:33.394
Edward Bloom, first son of Ashton...

275
00:26:33.566 --> 00:26:37.058
...it's with a heavy heart
that we see you go.

276
00:26:37.236 --> 00:26:39.898
But take with you
this key to the city...

277
00:26:40.072 --> 00:26:43.007
...and know that any time
you want to come back...

278
00:26:43.176 --> 00:26:46.236
...all our doors are open to you.

279
00:26:54.754 --> 00:26:58.781
<i>That afternoon as I left Ashton,</i>
<i>everyone seemed to have advice.</i>

280
00:26:58.958 --> 00:27:01.825
Find yourself a nice girl, now!

281
00:27:02.261 --> 00:27:05.253
Watch your pride, Edward Bloom!

282
00:27:05.464 --> 00:27:09.833
<i>But there was one person whose</i>
<i>counsel I held above all others.</i>

283
00:27:13.906 --> 00:27:16.636
<i>She said that the biggest fish</i>
<i>in the river...</i>

284
00:27:16.809 --> 00:27:19.972
<i>...gets that way</i>
<i>by never being caught.</i>

285
00:27:24.684 --> 00:27:28.051
-What'd she say?
-Beats me.

286
00:27:39.899 --> 00:27:41.730
<i>There were two roads out of Ashton:</i>

287
00:27:41.901 --> 00:27:44.802
<i>A new one which was paved</i>
<i>and an older one that wasn't.</i>

288
00:27:44.971 --> 00:27:46.962
<i>People didn't use</i>
<i>the old one anymore...</i>

289
00:27:47.139 --> 00:27:49.937
<i>...and it had developed a reputation</i>
<i>for being haunted.</i>

290
00:27:50.109 --> 00:27:53.306
<i>Well, since I had no intention</i>
<i>of ever returning to Ashton...</i>

291
00:27:53.479 --> 00:27:57.643
<i>...this seemed as good a time as any</i>
<i>to find out what lay down that old one.</i>

292
00:27:57.817 --> 00:28:02.550
-You know anyone who's taken it?
-That poet, Norther Winslow, did.

293
00:28:03.022 --> 00:28:05.490
He was going to Paris, France.

294
00:28:05.658 --> 00:28:09.094
I guess he liked it.
No one ever heard from him again.

295
00:28:09.328 --> 00:28:11.455
I'll tell you what.
You go the other way.

296
00:28:11.631 --> 00:28:15.192
I'll cut through here,
and I'll meet you on the far side.

297
00:28:16.402 --> 00:28:19.496
You're just trying to run away,
aren't you?

298
00:28:21.207 --> 00:28:22.435
Here.

299
00:28:23.743 --> 00:28:26.837
Just to be sure, you can take my pack.

300
00:29:06.018 --> 00:29:07.986
Why, you son of a...

301
00:29:33.946 --> 00:29:36.540
<i>Now, there comes a point</i>
<i>when a reasonable man...</i>

302
00:29:36.716 --> 00:29:41.483
<i>...will swallow his pride and admit</i>
<i>that he's made a terrible mistake.</i>

303
00:29:41.654 --> 00:29:46.591
<i>The truth is,</i>
<i>I was never a reasonable man.</i>

304
00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:50.456
<i>And what I recall of</i>
<i>Sunday school was that...</i>

305
00:29:50.629 --> 00:29:55.726
<i>...the more difficult something is,</i>
<i>the more rewarding it is in the end.</i>

306
00:30:54.994 --> 00:30:57.758
Friend! Welcome to you!

307
00:30:58.197 --> 00:31:01.189
-What's your name?
-Edward Bloom.

308
00:31:02.301 --> 00:31:05.395
-"Bloom" like a flower?
-Yes.

309
00:31:05.571 --> 00:31:09.735
Oh, here! Here you are, right here.
"Edward Bloom."

310
00:31:09.909 --> 00:31:12.241
We weren't expecting you yet.

311
00:31:12.411 --> 00:31:14.504
-You were expecting me?
-Not yet.

312
00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:19.344
-You must've taken a shortcut.
-Why, yes, I did. It almost killed me.

313
00:31:19.852 --> 00:31:23.982
Life will do that to you.
And truthfully, the long way is easier.

314
00:31:24.423 --> 00:31:27.290
-But it's longer.
-Much longer.

315
00:31:27.460 --> 00:31:30.395
And you're here now,
and that's what matters.

316
00:31:30.563 --> 00:31:31.791
What is this place?

317
00:31:31.964 --> 00:31:35.229
The town of Spectre.
Best-kept secret in Alabama!

318
00:31:35.401 --> 00:31:38.768
Now, it says here
you're from Ashton, right?

319
00:31:38.938 --> 00:31:42.271
Last person we had from Ashton
was Norther Winslow.

320
00:31:42.441 --> 00:31:44.375
The poet?
Whatever happened to him?

321
00:31:44.543 --> 00:31:48.912
He's still here. Let me buy you a drink.
I'll tell you all about it.

322
00:31:49.081 --> 00:31:51.015
Hell, I'll have him tell you!

323
00:31:51.183 --> 00:31:54.311
I have to meet somebody,
and I'm already running late.

324
00:31:54.487 --> 00:31:58.389
Now, son, I already told you.
You're early.

325
00:32:32.892 --> 00:32:36.020
Now, tell me if that isn't
the best pie you ever ate.

326
00:32:36.195 --> 00:32:37.890
It truly is.

327
00:32:42.234 --> 00:32:46.170
Well, everything tastes better here.
Even the water is sweet.

328
00:32:48.674 --> 00:32:51.939
Never gets too hot,
too cold, too humid.

329
00:32:52.444 --> 00:32:55.641
At night, the wind goes through
the trees where you'd swear...

330
00:32:55.814 --> 00:32:59.181
...there was a whole symphony
out there playing just for you.

331
00:33:02.688 --> 00:33:04.178
Hey! Jenny!

332
00:33:05.191 --> 00:33:06.988
Come back here!

333
00:33:12.698 --> 00:33:14.097
Jenny!

334
00:33:29.682 --> 00:33:31.445
Hey, I need those.

335
00:33:31.951 --> 00:33:35.318
-There is no softer ground than town.
-That almost rhymes.

336
00:33:35.487 --> 00:33:37.978
He's our poet laureate. Come on.

337
00:33:41.527 --> 00:33:45.019
<i>I agreed to spend the afternoon,</i>
<i>if only to understand...</i>

338
00:33:45.197 --> 00:33:50.066
<i>...the mystery of how a place could</i>
<i>feel so strange and yet so familiar.</i>

339
00:33:53.973 --> 00:33:56.533
I've been working on this poem
for 12 years.

340
00:33:56.709 --> 00:33:58.301
Really?

341
00:33:58.477 --> 00:34:03.278
There's a lot of expectation.
I don't wanna disappoint my fans.

342
00:34:04.183 --> 00:34:05.844
May I?

343
00:34:26.105 --> 00:34:27.766
It's only three lines long.

344
00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:34.341
This is why you should never show
a work in progress.

345
00:35:30.035 --> 00:35:32.003
I got him!

346
00:35:47.619 --> 00:35:49.280
There's leeches in there.

347
00:35:50.222 --> 00:35:53.248
-Did you see that woman?
-What did she look like?

348
00:35:53.425 --> 00:35:56.883
-Well, she was...
-Was she naked?

349
00:35:59.231 --> 00:36:00.755
Yes, she was.

350
00:36:00.933 --> 00:36:05.199
It's not a woman. It's a fish.
No one ever catches her.

351
00:36:05.371 --> 00:36:07.862
Fish looks different
to different people.

352
00:36:08.173 --> 00:36:11.973
My daddy said it looked like the
coon dog he had when he was a kid...

353
00:36:12.378 --> 00:36:14.869
...back from the dead.

354
00:36:18.283 --> 00:36:20.148
Oh, darn.

355
00:36:22.087 --> 00:36:24.317
-How old are you?
-Eighteen.

356
00:36:24.490 --> 00:36:28.392
I'm 8. That means when I'm 18,
you'll be 28.

357
00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:33.327
-And when I'm 28, you'll only be 38.
-You're pretty good at arithmetic.

358
00:36:33.499 --> 00:36:37.993
And when I'm 38, you'll be 48.
That's not much difference at all.

359
00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:40.968
Sure is a lot now, though, huh?

360
00:37:14.740 --> 00:37:17.368
Hey, Edward!

361
00:37:26.218 --> 00:37:30.279
<i>Roses are red</i>
<i>Violets are blue</i>

362
00:37:30.456 --> 00:37:32.981
<i>I love Spectre...</i>

363
00:37:38.730 --> 00:37:40.721
Excuse me.

364
00:37:46.905 --> 00:37:50.341
Jenny thinks you're quite a catch.
We all do.

365
00:37:51.176 --> 00:37:52.507
What?

366
00:37:52.678 --> 00:37:55.909
I said, you're quite a catch.

367
00:38:18.170 --> 00:38:19.933
I have to leave.

368
00:38:23.976 --> 00:38:25.341
Tonight.

369
00:38:25.811 --> 00:38:27.073
Why?

370
00:38:28.447 --> 00:38:30.972
This town is more than any man
could ask for.

371
00:38:31.149 --> 00:38:34.676
And if I were to end up here,
I would consider myself lucky.

372
00:38:34.853 --> 00:38:38.755
But the truth is, I'm just not ready
to end up anywhere.

373
00:38:39.358 --> 00:38:41.053
But no one's ever left.

374
00:38:42.294 --> 00:38:45.627
How are you gonna make it
without your shoes?

375
00:38:45.797 --> 00:38:48.425
Well, I suspect it will hurt. A lot.

376
00:38:52.371 --> 00:38:55.033
Now, I'm sorry, but...
Well, goodbye.

377
00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:02.373
-You won't find a better place.
-I don't expect to.

378
00:39:07.519 --> 00:39:09.544
Promise me you'll come back.

379
00:39:11.023 --> 00:39:15.824
I promise. Someday.
When I'm really supposed to.

380
00:39:46.792 --> 00:39:49.625
<i>That night,</i>
<i>I reached two conclusions.</i>

381
00:39:50.429 --> 00:39:55.457
<i>The first was that a dangerous path</i>
<i>is made much worse by darkness.</i>

382
00:39:55.634 --> 00:40:00.367
<i>The second was that I was</i>
<i>hopelessly and irrevocably lost.</i>

383
00:40:00.939 --> 00:40:03.931
<i>These woods would become</i>
<i>my graveyard.</i>

384
00:40:06.178 --> 00:40:11.081
<i>As difficult as it was to reach Spectre,</i>
<i>I was fated to get there eventually.</i>

385
00:40:11.249 --> 00:40:14.650
<i>After all, no man can avoid</i>
<i>reaching the end of his life.</i>

386
00:40:29.301 --> 00:40:33.567
<i>And then I realized</i>
<i>this wasn't the end of my life.</i>

387
00:40:33.739 --> 00:40:36.731
This isn't how I die.

388
00:40:54.226 --> 00:40:55.557
Friend.

389
00:40:55.727 --> 00:40:59.663
-What happened to your shoes?
-They kind of got ahead of me.

390
00:41:16.581 --> 00:41:19.607
I don't know if you saw it,
but Josephine had some photos...

391
00:41:19.785 --> 00:41:21.343
...in the most recent <i>Newsweek.</i>

392
00:41:23.889 --> 00:41:25.379
Really?

393
00:41:25.557 --> 00:41:26.888
That's just wonderful.

394
00:41:28.727 --> 00:41:33.221
I spent a week in Morocco
for the story. It was incredible.

395
00:41:33.398 --> 00:41:37.858
Oh. We'll have to pick up a copy.

396
00:41:48.213 --> 00:41:51.273
I don't know if you're aware of this,
Josephine...

397
00:41:51.750 --> 00:41:56.915
...but African parrots, in their native
Congo, they speak only French.

398
00:41:58.023 --> 00:41:59.285
Really?

399
00:41:59.891 --> 00:42:03.190
You're lucky to get four words
out of them in English.

400
00:42:03.361 --> 00:42:06.262
But if you were to walk
through the jungle...

401
00:42:06.431 --> 00:42:10.128
...you'd hear them speaking
the most elaborate French.

402
00:42:10.836 --> 00:42:15.967
Those parrots talk about everything.
Politics, movies, fashion.

403
00:42:16.141 --> 00:42:18.837
Everything but religion.

404
00:42:19.945 --> 00:42:21.276
Why not religion, Dad?

405
00:42:21.980 --> 00:42:26.644
It's rude to talk about religion. You
never know who you're gonna offend.

406
00:42:31.990 --> 00:42:35.187
Josephine actually went
to the Congo last year.

407
00:42:36.828 --> 00:42:38.887
Oh, so you know.

408
00:42:54.546 --> 00:42:56.980
Hello.

409
00:42:57.149 --> 00:42:59.515
Hi. How are you feeling?

410
00:43:00.485 --> 00:43:03.818
Oh, I was dreaming.

411
00:43:04.356 --> 00:43:06.847
What were you dreaming about?

412
00:43:07.926 --> 00:43:10.952
Oh, I don't usually remember...

413
00:43:11.129 --> 00:43:15.463
...unless they're especially portentous.
Do you know what that word means?

414
00:43:15.767 --> 00:43:18.895
It means when you dream about
something that's gonna happen.

415
00:43:19.638 --> 00:43:22.129
Like one night, I had a dream...

416
00:43:22.307 --> 00:43:27.074
...where this crow came and said:

417
00:43:27.245 --> 00:43:29.736
"Your aunt is gonna die."

418
00:43:30.916 --> 00:43:34.147
I was so scared,
I woke up my parents...

419
00:43:34.319 --> 00:43:38.551
...but they said it was just a dream
and to get back to bed.

420
00:43:38.723 --> 00:43:42.215
But the next morning,
my Aunt Stacy was dead.

421
00:43:42.594 --> 00:43:44.562
That's terrible.

422
00:43:44.729 --> 00:43:49.860
Terrible for her, but think about me,
young boy with that kind of power.

423
00:43:50.435 --> 00:43:55.168
Wasn't three weeks later when the
crow came back to me in a dream...

424
00:43:55.340 --> 00:44:00.039
...and said, "Your daddy's gonna die."

425
00:44:00.879 --> 00:44:02.938
I didn't know what to do.

426
00:44:03.315 --> 00:44:06.978
I finally told my father, but he said:

427
00:44:07.152 --> 00:44:12.988
"Oh, not to worry."
But I could see he was rattled.

428
00:44:13.158 --> 00:44:15.991
The next morning, he wasn't himself.

429
00:44:16.161 --> 00:44:20.757
Kept looking around, waiting for
something to drop on his head.

430
00:44:20.932 --> 00:44:26.029
Because the crow didn't say how it
was gonna happen, just those words:

431
00:44:26.204 --> 00:44:28.434
"Your daddy's gonna die."

432
00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:34.945
Well, he left home early
and was gone a long time.

433
00:44:35.113 --> 00:44:37.843
When he finally came back,
he looked terrible...

434
00:44:38.016 --> 00:44:41.281
...like he was waiting
for the ax to fall all day.

435
00:44:42.320 --> 00:44:44.083
He said to my mother:

436
00:44:44.256 --> 00:44:48.022
"I've just had the worst day of my life."

437
00:44:48.193 --> 00:44:52.186
"You think you've had a bad day?"
She said.

438
00:44:52.364 --> 00:44:57.495
"This morning, the milkman
dropped dead on the porch."

439
00:44:59.638 --> 00:45:03.836
Because, see, my mother
was banging the milkman.

440
00:45:05.644 --> 00:45:10.308
-Can I take your picture?
-Oh, you don't need a picture.

441
00:45:10.482 --> 00:45:14.680
Just look up the word "handsome"
in the dictionary.

442
00:45:14.853 --> 00:45:16.184
Please?

443
00:45:17.822 --> 00:45:19.289
All right.

444
00:45:22.694 --> 00:45:24.753
I have photos of the wedding
to show you.

445
00:45:24.930 --> 00:45:27.364
There is a great one
of you and my father.

446
00:45:33.705 --> 00:45:38.335
I want to see pictures of your wedding.
I've never seen any.

447
00:45:38.510 --> 00:45:41.946
That's because we didn't have
a proper wedding.

448
00:45:42.113 --> 00:45:45.514
