607
01:02:19.910 --> 01:02:21.775
Well...

608
01:02:23.914 --> 01:02:25.438
Congratulations.

609
01:02:28.686 --> 01:02:31.211
I'm sorry to have bothered you.

610
01:02:36.160 --> 01:02:39.254
Stop it! It's not funny.

611
01:02:39.964 --> 01:02:42.432
That poor boy.

612
01:02:43.033 --> 01:02:46.230
<i>Fate has a cruel way</i>
<i>of circling around on you.</i>

613
01:02:46.403 --> 01:02:48.462
<i>After all this work to leave Ashton...</i>

614
01:02:48.639 --> 01:02:54.100
<i>...the girl I love was now engaged</i>
<i>to one of its biggest jerks.</i>

615
01:02:54.278 --> 01:02:56.508
<i>There's a time when a man</i>
<i>needs to fight...</i>

616
01:02:56.680 --> 01:03:00.138
<i>...and a time when he needs to accept</i>
<i>that his destiny's lost...</i>

617
01:03:00.317 --> 01:03:04.413
<i>...the ship has sailed,</i>
<i>and that only a fool will continue.</i>

618
01:03:04.588 --> 01:03:07.887
<i>Truth is, I've always been a fool.</i>

619
01:03:08.926 --> 01:03:13.863
Sandra Templeton, I love you,
and I will marry you!

620
01:03:18.369 --> 01:03:21.566
So as you can see, if we apply
these rules to our everyday life...

621
01:03:22.239 --> 01:03:25.003
...supply and demand
makes much more sense.

622
01:03:25.175 --> 01:03:29.202
Take a look at the next graph, and the
import of this will be even stronger...

623
01:03:38.288 --> 01:03:40.950
...for three hours.

624
01:03:41.125 --> 01:03:42.990
Look!

625
01:04:13.524 --> 01:04:16.789
-Daffodils!
-They're your favorite flower.

626
01:04:16.960 --> 01:04:18.393
How did you get so many?

627
01:04:19.096 --> 01:04:21.257
I called everywhere in five states.

628
01:04:21.432 --> 01:04:24.401
I told them it was the only way
to get my wife to marry me.

629
01:04:26.603 --> 01:04:28.298
You don't even know me.

630
01:04:28.939 --> 01:04:31.407
I have the rest of my life to find out.

631
01:04:32.543 --> 01:04:34.204
Sandra!

632
01:04:36.080 --> 01:04:38.640
It's Don. Promise you won't hurt him.

633
01:04:38.816 --> 01:04:41.751
If that's what you want, I swear to it.

634
01:04:42.186 --> 01:04:44.620
-Bloom?
-Don.

635
01:04:45.422 --> 01:04:49.756
What the hell are you doing?
This is my girl. Mine!

636
01:04:49.927 --> 01:04:52.521
I wasn't aware
that she belonged to anyone.

637
01:04:55.799 --> 01:04:58.791
What's the matter?
Are you too scared to fight back?

638
01:04:58.969 --> 01:05:00.630
I promised I wouldn't.

639
01:05:01.839 --> 01:05:04.137
Stop it!

640
01:05:04.641 --> 01:05:05.938
Don, stop!

641
01:05:06.110 --> 01:05:11.514
<i>While I took the beating of a lifetime,</i>
<i>Don Price was ultimately defeated.</i>

642
01:05:11.682 --> 01:05:16.449
<i>All the physical activity had worsened</i>
<i>a congenital valve defect.</i>

643
01:05:16.620 --> 01:05:18.781
<i>Put simply, his heart</i>
<i>wasn't strong enough.</i>

644
01:05:31.368 --> 01:05:33.836
Don! I will never marry you.

645
01:05:34.738 --> 01:05:37.434
What?
You mean, you love this guy?

646
01:05:37.641 --> 01:05:40.769
He's almost a stranger,
and I prefer him to you.

647
01:06:08.972 --> 01:06:14.342
<i>As it turned out, Sandra was able to</i>
<i>keep her same date at the chapel.</i>

648
01:06:14.511 --> 01:06:17.708
<i>Only the groom had changed.</i>

649
01:06:18.682 --> 01:06:22.709
I thought you said you didn't
have a church wedding.

650
01:06:22.886 --> 01:06:25.980
We were all set to,
but there was a complication.

651
01:06:29.826 --> 01:06:33.557
Is it the medicine
that's making you thirsty?

652
01:06:34.164 --> 01:06:37.691
Truth is,
I've been thirsty my whole life.

653
01:06:38.835 --> 01:06:41.360
Never really known why.

654
01:06:42.172 --> 01:06:44.800
There was a time when I was 11...

655
01:06:44.975 --> 01:06:46.909
You were talking about your wedding.

656
01:06:47.077 --> 01:06:51.013
I didn't forget.
I was just working on a tangent.

657
01:06:51.181 --> 01:06:54.844
You see, most men, they'll tell you
a story straight through.

658
01:06:55.018 --> 01:06:58.749
It won't be complicated,
but it won't be interesting, either.

659
01:06:59.389 --> 01:06:59.890
I like your stories.

660
01:06:59.890 --> 01:07:00.390
I like your stories.

661
01:07:00.390 --> 01:07:00.891
I like your stories.

662
01:07:00.891 --> 01:07:01.391
And I like you.

663
01:07:01.391 --> 01:07:02.893
And I like you.

664
01:07:02.893 --> 01:07:03.727
Well, when you work for the circus,
you don't have a regular address.

665
01:07:03.727 --> 01:07:07.322
Well, when you work for the circus,
you don't have a regular address.

666
01:07:07.497 --> 01:07:10.660
So after three years,
there was a lot of undelivered mail.

667
01:07:11.602 --> 01:07:14.298
During the four weeks
I was in the hospital...

668
01:07:14.471 --> 01:07:17.133
...the postmaster
finally caught up with me.

669
01:07:17.307 --> 01:07:20.834
And it seems that while my heart
belonged to Sandra...

670
01:07:21.011 --> 01:07:26.643
...the rest of my body belonged
to the U.S. Government.

671
01:07:43.634 --> 01:07:47.297
A hitch in the Army was up to
three years at that point...

672
01:07:47.471 --> 01:07:50.497
...and having waited three years
just to meet Sandra...

673
01:07:50.674 --> 01:07:54.633
...I knew I couldn't survive
being away from her that long.

674
01:07:54.811 --> 01:07:57.939
So I took every hazardous
assignment I could find...

675
01:07:58.115 --> 01:08:02.449
...with the hope of getting
my time down to less than a year.

676
01:08:02.619 --> 01:08:05.144
Go! Go!

677
01:08:07.724 --> 01:08:09.783
When I was offered
a secret mission...

678
01:08:09.960 --> 01:08:13.919
...to steal the plans for
the Wong Kai Tang power plant...

679
01:08:14.097 --> 01:08:17.225
...I jumped at the chance
to serve my country.

680
01:08:20.304 --> 01:08:22.864
Go! Go!

681
01:10:18.221 --> 01:10:20.689
Any of you got needs?

682
01:12:06.630 --> 01:12:08.655
How could you miss your cue?

683
01:12:08.832 --> 01:12:12.791
You make me look like a fool
out there alone.

684
01:12:13.136 --> 01:12:14.660
You weren't alone!

685
01:12:16.840 --> 01:12:18.034
Who the hell are you?

686
01:12:18.208 --> 01:12:20.039
I'm not going to hurt you.

687
01:12:20.210 --> 01:12:21.199
Damn right you're not.

688
01:12:21.378 --> 01:12:22.367
GUARD!

689
01:12:24.214 --> 01:12:25.875
Tell your men not to bother us!

690
01:12:26.049 --> 01:12:27.346
And close that curtain!

691
01:12:30.086 --> 01:12:32.213
Please, I need your help.

692
01:12:32.856 --> 01:12:35.347
What makes you think we'll help you?

693
01:12:43.967 --> 01:12:49.064
<i>Over the next hour, I described</i>
<i>my love for Sandra K. Templeton...</i>

694
01:12:49.239 --> 01:12:52.140
<i>...and the ordeal that brought me</i>
<i>before them.</i>

695
01:12:52.309 --> 01:12:55.676
<i>As it had always been,</i>
<i>this love was my salvation.</i>

696
01:12:55.845 --> 01:12:58.006
<i>It was destined to be.</i>

697
01:12:58.181 --> 01:13:01.344
<i>We put together an elaborate</i>
<i>plan for escape...</i>

698
01:13:01.518 --> 01:13:03.748
<i>...involving a whaling ship</i>
<i>to Russia...</i>

699
01:13:03.920 --> 01:13:07.981
<i>...a barge to Cuba,</i>
<i>and a small, dirty canoe to Miami.</i>

700
01:13:08.158 --> 01:13:10.649
<i>We all knew it would be dangerous.</i>

701
01:13:11.895 --> 01:13:14.363
And what are we supposed to do
when we get to America?

702
01:13:15.131 --> 01:13:16.428
I can get you bookings.

703
01:13:16.600 --> 01:13:19.364
I know the biggest man
in show business.

704
01:13:20.470 --> 01:13:21.494
Bob Hope?!

705
01:13:21.871 --> 01:13:22.860
Bigger.

706
01:13:26.643 --> 01:13:27.632
Ready?

707
01:13:31.047 --> 01:13:34.915
<i>And so the twins and I
<i>began our arduous journey...</i>

708
01:13:35.085 --> 01:13:37.019
<i>...halfway around the world.</i>

709
01:13:37.187 --> 01:13:41.556
<i>Unfortunately, there was no way</i>
<i>to send a message back to America.</i>

710
01:13:41.725 --> 01:13:45.718
<i>And so it was no surprise</i>
<i>that the Army believed I was dead.</i>

711
01:14:01.978 --> 01:14:04.503
No, God!

712
01:14:06.149 --> 01:14:10.518
<i>After four months, Sandra had gotten</i>
<i>over the worst of the nightmares.</i>

713
01:14:10.687 --> 01:14:15.124
<i>When the phone rang, she didn't think</i>
<i>it was somehow me calling her.</i>

714
01:14:15.291 --> 01:14:20.126
<i>When a car drove past, she didn't</i>
<i>get up to check out the window.</i>

715
01:14:55.198 --> 01:14:58.292
I talked with your father last night.

716
01:15:01.771 --> 01:15:04.763
You never told me
how your parents met.

717
01:15:04.941 --> 01:15:06.772
They met at Auburn.

718
01:15:06.943 --> 01:15:10.811
What about the details?
How they fell in love.

719
01:15:10.980 --> 01:15:12.914
The circus. The war.

720
01:15:13.083 --> 01:15:14.983
You never told me any of that.

721
01:15:15.151 --> 01:15:17.312
That's because most of it
never happened.

722
01:15:19.155 --> 01:15:21.385
But it's romantic.

723
01:15:24.794 --> 01:15:27.354
-What?
-What, I know better...

724
01:15:27.530 --> 01:15:30.693
...than to argue romance
with a French woman.

725
01:15:32.435 --> 01:15:34.426
Do you love your father?

726
01:15:34.604 --> 01:15:38.199
Everyone loves my father.
He's a very likable guy.

727
01:15:39.576 --> 01:15:41.134
Do you love him?

728
01:15:46.282 --> 01:15:48.273
You have to understand.

729
01:15:48.451 --> 01:15:52.217
When I was growing up,
he was gone more than he was there.

730
01:15:52.388 --> 01:15:56.347
And I started thinking maybe he's got
this second life somewhere else.

731
01:15:57.260 --> 01:15:59.990
Another family, another house.

732
01:16:00.163 --> 01:16:03.462
And he leaves us
and goes to them.

733
01:16:03.633 --> 01:16:05.260
Or, or...

734
01:16:07.670 --> 01:16:11.766
Or maybe there is no second family.
Maybe he never wanted a family.

735
01:16:12.809 --> 01:16:17.405
Whatever it is, he likes his second life
better and he tells his stories...

736
01:16:17.580 --> 01:16:20.071
...because he can't stand
this boring place.

737
01:16:20.250 --> 01:16:23.344
-But it's not true.
-Well, what's true?

738
01:16:25.421 --> 01:16:28.219
He's never told me
a single true thing.

739
01:16:30.160 --> 01:16:31.684
Look, hey...

740
01:16:33.096 --> 01:16:35.894
I know why you like him.

741
01:16:36.065 --> 01:16:38.829
I know why everyone likes him.

742
01:16:39.969 --> 01:16:43.268
But I need you to tell me
that I'm not crazy.

743
01:16:43.439 --> 01:16:46.340
Oh, you're not.

744
01:16:48.344 --> 01:16:51.040
And I think you should talk to him.

745
01:16:56.252 --> 01:16:59.415
<i>Larry Puckett's Chevrolet</i>
<i>lets the customer do the talking.</i>

746
01:16:59.589 --> 01:17:03.252
<i>Larry Puckett's GM-certified vehicles</i>
<i>are under factory warranty.</i>

747
01:17:03.426 --> 01:17:07.453
<i>And you can save up to 40 percent</i>
<i>off the original MSRP.</i>

748
01:17:07.630 --> 01:17:10.326
-Did I ever tell you about...?
-Yes.

749
01:17:11.568 --> 01:17:15.004
The maple tree and the Buick?
We've heard it.

750
01:17:17.440 --> 01:17:20.932
I know someone who hasn't.

751
01:17:21.110 --> 01:17:24.739
-The...
-Tree fell on the car, spilling the syrup.

752
01:17:24.914 --> 01:17:28.441
Which attracted the flies,
which got stuck to it...

753
01:17:28.618 --> 01:17:31.678
...and flew off with the whole car.

754
01:17:36.192 --> 01:17:39.650
But the real story
is how I got the car.

755
01:17:39.829 --> 01:17:41.524
-You see...
-Dad?

756
01:17:41.698 --> 01:17:43.165
Son.

757
01:17:43.333 --> 01:17:45.358
Can we talk?

758
01:17:47.804 --> 01:17:50.238
I think I'll get started
on these dishes.

759
01:17:50.406 --> 01:17:52.601
I'll help you.

760
01:18:08.157 --> 01:18:11.490
-You know about icebergs, Dad?
-Do I?

761
01:18:11.661 --> 01:18:14.095
I saw an iceberg once.

762
01:18:14.264 --> 01:18:17.563
They were hauling it down to Texas
for drinking water.

763
01:18:17.734 --> 01:18:21.795
They didn't count on there being
an elephant frozen inside.

764
01:18:21.971 --> 01:18:24.769
-The woolly kind. A mammoth.
-Dad!

765
01:18:24.941 --> 01:18:26.909
What?

766
01:18:28.711 --> 01:18:30.906
I'm trying to make a metaphor here.

767
01:18:31.080 --> 01:18:33.776
Well, you shouldn't have started
with a question...

768
01:18:33.950 --> 01:18:36.248
...because people
want to answer questions.

769
01:18:36.419 --> 01:18:40.014
You should've started with,
"The thing about icebergs is..."

770
01:18:40.189 --> 01:18:43.625
Okay, okay. The thing about
icebergs is you only see 10 percent.

771
01:18:43.793 --> 01:18:47.194
The other 90 percent is below
the water where you can't see it.

772
01:18:47.363 --> 01:18:49.422
And that's...

773
01:18:49.599 --> 01:18:52.363
...what it is with you, Dad.

774
01:18:52.535 --> 01:18:55.265
I am only seeing this little bit...

775
01:18:55.438 --> 01:18:57.269
...that sticks above the water.

776
01:18:57.440 --> 01:19:00.603
Oh, you're only seeing down
to my nose? My chin? My...

777
01:19:00.777 --> 01:19:03.712
Dad, I have no idea who you are...

778
01:19:03.880 --> 01:19:07.111
...because you've never told me
a single fact.

779
01:19:07.283 --> 01:19:11.549
I've told you a thousand facts, Will.
That's what I do, I tell stories!

780
01:19:11.721 --> 01:19:14.952
You tell... lies, Dad.

781
01:19:15.124 --> 01:19:16.455
You tell amusing lies.

782
01:19:16.626 --> 01:19:19.720
Stories are what you tell
a 5-year-old at bedtime.

783
01:19:19.896 --> 01:19:22.763
They're not elaborate mythologies
that you maintain...

784
01:19:22.932 --> 01:19:25.366
...when your son is 10 and 15
and 20 and 30.

785
01:19:25.535 --> 01:19:28.402
And I believed you.

786
01:19:30.173 --> 01:19:33.609
I believed your stories
so much longer than I should have.

787
01:19:33.776 --> 01:19:36.870
Then when I realized
everything you said was impossible...

788
01:19:37.046 --> 01:19:39.276
...I felt like a fool
to have trusted you.

789
01:19:39.449 --> 01:19:41.713
You're like Santa Claus
and the Easter Bunny.

790
01:19:41.884 --> 01:19:43.715
Just as charming and just as fake.

791
01:19:43.886 --> 01:19:46.116
You think I'm fake.

792
01:19:47.590 --> 01:19:51.720
Only on the surface, Dad.
But it's all I've ever seen.

793
01:19:53.496 --> 01:19:55.157
Look.

794
01:19:56.733 --> 01:19:59.167
I'm about to have...

795
01:19:59.669 --> 01:20:00.897
...a kid of my own.

796
01:20:01.070 --> 01:20:03.766
It would kill me
if he went through his whole life...

797
01:20:03.940 --> 01:20:05.999
...never understanding me.

798
01:20:06.175 --> 01:20:08.609
It would kill you, huh?

799
01:20:10.580 --> 01:20:14.107
What do you want, Will?
Who do you want me to be?

800
01:20:14.283 --> 01:20:15.944
Just yourself.

801
01:20:16.119 --> 01:20:19.919
Good, bad, everything.
Just show me who you are for once.

802
01:20:20.089 --> 01:20:23.183
I've been nothing but myself
since the day I was born.

803
01:20:23.359 --> 01:20:27.887
And if you can't see that,
it's your failing, not mine!

804
01:20:59.228 --> 01:21:03.790
Your father decided
that he needed to have an office...

805
01:21:03.966 --> 01:21:07.060
...and, of course, it wouldn't do
to have it in the house.

806
01:21:07.236 --> 01:21:08.760
So...

807
01:21:10.406 --> 01:21:14.137
You'll know better than me
what's important.

808
01:21:25.455 --> 01:21:27.582
What is it?

809
01:21:29.025 --> 01:21:31.016
It was during the war.

810
01:21:31.194 --> 01:21:35.062
Your father went missing.
They thought he was dead.

811
01:21:36.265 --> 01:21:38.665
Oh, that really happened?

812
01:21:38.835 --> 01:21:42.601
Not everything your father says
is a complete fabrication.

813
01:21:43.940 --> 01:21:46.966
I think I'll go check on him.

814
01:21:49.145 --> 01:21:51.807
I need to lie down for a bit.

815
01:21:51.981 --> 01:21:54.609
Okay, go.

816
01:22:09.398 --> 01:22:14.165
<i>After the war, the sons of Alabama</i>
<i>returned home, looking for work.</i>

817
01:22:14.337 --> 01:22:16.100
<i>Each had the advantage over me.</i>

818
01:22:16.272 --> 01:22:19.833
<i>They were alive,</i>
<i>while I was officially deceased.</i>

819
01:22:20.009 --> 01:22:21.772
<i>With my prospects few...</i>

820
01:22:21.944 --> 01:22:25.744
<i>...I took a job as a traveling salesman.</i>
<i>It suited me.</i>

821
01:22:25.915 --> 01:22:29.043
<i>If there was one thing</i>
<i>you can say about Edward Bloom...</i>

822
01:22:29.218 --> 01:22:31.448
<i>...it's that I am a social person.</i>

823
01:22:32.155 --> 01:22:35.556
-Congratulations.
-Thank you, sir.

824
01:22:36.225 --> 01:22:38.819
<i>I could be gone</i>
<i>for weeks at a time.</i>

825
01:22:38.995 --> 01:22:41.930
<i>But every other Friday,</i>
<i>I put all the money I made...</i>

826
01:22:42.098 --> 01:22:45.727
<i>...into an account set aside</i>
<i>for a proper house...</i>

827
01:22:45.902 --> 01:22:48.097
<i>...with a white picket fence.</i>

828
01:22:53.876 --> 01:22:57.403
I'd like a moment of your time
to tell you about my new product...

829
01:22:57.580 --> 01:22:59.912
...the Handi-matic.

830
01:23:15.398 --> 01:23:18.697
<i>A few years later, I added</i>
<i>other products and other cities...</i>

831
01:23:18.868 --> 01:23:22.429
<i>...until my territory stretched from</i>
<i>the coast to western Texas.</i>

832
01:23:47.296 --> 01:23:48.786
Edward?

833
01:23:48.965 --> 01:23:50.592
Edward Bloom!

834
01:23:50.766 --> 01:23:52.393
It's me, Norther Winslow.

835
01:23:52.568 --> 01:23:54.433
I don't believe it.

836
01:23:54.604 --> 01:23:58.904
<i>I was astonished to see the greatest</i>
<i>poet of both Ashton and Spectre...</i>

837
01:23:59.075 --> 01:24:00.975
<i>...all the way out in Texas.</i>

838
01:24:01.143 --> 01:24:05.011
I want you to know, when you left
Spectre, it opened my eyes.

839
01:24:05.181 --> 01:24:08.844
There was a whole life out there
that I was not living.

840
01:24:09.018 --> 01:24:10.508
So I traveled.

841
01:24:10.686 --> 01:24:13.849
I saw France, Africa,
half of South America.

842
01:24:14.023 --> 01:24:16.651
Every day, a new adventure.
That's my motto.

843
01:24:16.826 --> 01:24:18.987
That's great, Norther.
I'm happy for you.

844
01:24:19.161 --> 01:24:21.425
What are you doing now?

845
01:24:22.531 --> 01:24:24.158
I'm robbing this place.

846
01:24:28.204 --> 01:24:30.172
All right, everybody down!

847
01:24:31.641 --> 01:24:34.906
Hey!
Just slide that over.

848
01:24:41.117 --> 01:24:43.881
-Would you mind grabbing that?
-What?

849
01:24:44.320 --> 01:24:45.810
The gun.

850
01:24:47.323 --> 01:24:49.757
I'm gonna be cleaning out
the cash drawers...

851
01:24:49.925 --> 01:24:54.123
...and my associate here
is gonna handle the vault.

852
01:25:00.136 --> 01:25:03.594
All right, you!
You help my friend, okay?

853
01:25:04.407 --> 01:25:06.068
Let's go!

854
01:25:25.728 --> 01:25:27.662
I'm sorry, ma'am.
I am.

855
01:25:27.830 --> 01:25:30.958
I really just don't want anyone
to get hurt.

856
01:25:31.133 --> 01:25:33.067
It's not that.

857
01:25:34.036 --> 01:25:35.970
It's just...

858
01:25:41.444 --> 01:25:43.036
There's no money.

859
01:25:43.846 --> 01:25:47.714
We're completely bankrupt.
Don't tell anybody.

860
01:25:47.883 --> 01:25:51.876
<i>It turned out that the Savings & Loan</i>
<i>had already been robbed...</i>

861
01:25:52.054 --> 01:25:56.548
<i>...not by armed bandits, but</i>
<i>by speculators in Texas real estate.</i>

862
01:25:56.726 --> 01:25:58.717
All right, let's go!

863
01:26:09.905 --> 01:26:11.930
Norther!

864
01:26:31.560 --> 01:26:34.324
Yeah! There's gotta be
close to $400 here!

865
01:26:34.497 --> 01:26:36.988
And that's just from the drawers.

866
01:26:37.166 --> 01:26:39.634
Let's see what you got
from the vault.

867
01:26:49.545 --> 01:26:52.139
This is it? The whole vault?

868
01:26:52.314 --> 01:26:54.680
I'm afraid so.

869
01:26:57.420 --> 01:26:59.354
It's got your deposit slip on it.

870
01:26:59.522 --> 01:27:02.389
Well, I just didn't want you
leaving empty-handed.

871
01:27:02.558 --> 01:27:06.324
There's something you should know.
The reason they don't have money...

872
01:27:06.495 --> 01:27:09.760
<i>I told Norther about</i>
<i>the vagaries of Texas oil money...</i>

873
01:27:09.932 --> 01:27:12.799
<i>...and its effect on real-estate prices...</i>

874
01:27:12.968 --> 01:27:16.131
<i>...and how lax enforcement</i>
<i>of fiduciary process...</i>

875
01:27:16.305 --> 01:27:20.105
<i>...had made savings and loans</i>
<i>particularly vulnerable.</i>

876
01:27:20.276 --> 01:27:25.077
<i>Hearing this news,</i>
<i>Norther was left with one conclusion:</i>

877
01:27:25.247 --> 01:27:28.444
I should go to Wall Street.
That's where all the money is.

878
01:27:28.617 --> 01:27:32.212
<i>I knew then that while my days</i>
<i>as a criminal were over...</i>

879
01:27:32.388 --> 01:27:33.650
Thanks for the hand!

880
01:27:33.823 --> 01:27:35.814
<i>...Norther's were just beginning.</i>

881
01:27:35.991 --> 01:27:38.425
<i>When Norther made</i>
<i>his first million dollars...</i>

882
01:27:38.594 --> 01:27:40.858
<i>...he sent me a check for 10,000.</i>

883
01:27:41.030 --> 01:27:45.126
<i>I protested, but he said it was my fee</i>
<i>as his career advisor.</i>

884
01:27:45.301 --> 01:27:48.464
<i>It was enough to buy my wife</i>
<i>a proper house...</i>

885
01:27:48.637 --> 01:27:50.696
<i>...with a white picket fence.</i>

886
01:27:50.873 --> 01:27:55.071
<i>And for that, it was all the riches</i>
<i>a man could ever want.</i>

887
01:28:28.944 --> 01:28:30.878
I was drying out.

888
01:28:32.648 --> 01:28:34.548
I see.

889
01:28:36.719 --> 01:28:40.917
I think we ought to get you
a plant mister...

890
01:28:41.090 --> 01:28:43.888
...so we can just spray you
like a fern.

891
01:29:09.818 --> 01:29:12.286
Come, now.

892
01:29:16.625 --> 01:29:20.026
I don't think I'll ever dry out.

893
01:30:18.220 --> 01:30:21.246
You carry on. I'll see...

894
01:30:25.928 --> 01:30:27.725
Hello.

895
01:30:28.297 --> 01:30:32.233
-Are you Jennifer Hill?
-I am.

896
01:30:32.401 --> 01:30:34.562
And you're Will.

897
01:30:34.737 --> 01:30:37.706
I've seen a picture of you.
That's how I recognize you.

898
01:30:40.409 --> 01:30:43.071
Listen, Kenny,
why don't we skip the lesson today?

899
01:30:43.245 --> 01:30:45.543
We can go again next week.

900
01:30:45.714 --> 01:30:47.739
Do I have to give it back to my mom?

901
01:30:47.916 --> 01:30:50.476
Well, I won't tell her if you won't.

902
01:31:01.930 --> 01:31:03.397
How did you know my father?

903
01:31:03.799 --> 01:31:05.858
Well, this was on his sales route...

904
01:31:06.035 --> 01:31:10.096
...so he was through here all the time,
and everyone in town knew him.

905
01:31:10.272 --> 01:31:12.331
Were you having an affair?

906
01:31:14.743 --> 01:31:17.177
Wow. Wow, you just said it.

907
01:31:17.980 --> 01:31:21.609
I was expecting to dance
around this for another half-hour.

908
01:31:23.419 --> 01:31:26.445
I've seen him with women.
He flirts. He always has.

909
01:31:26.622 --> 01:31:30.752
And on some level,
I just presumed that he was...

910
01:31:30.926 --> 01:31:34.453
...cheating on my mom.
I just never had proof.

911
01:31:37.132 --> 01:31:39.032
Well, can I ask you a question?

912
01:31:39.201 --> 01:31:41.931
If you found this deed,
why didn't you just ask Eddie?

913
01:31:43.072 --> 01:31:45.404
Because he's dying.

914
01:31:54.116 --> 01:31:58.780
Look, I don't know how much
you want to know about any of this.

915
01:31:58.954 --> 01:32:01.081
You have one image of your father...

916
01:32:01.256 --> 01:32:03.520
...and it'd be wrong of me
to change it...

917
01:32:03.692 --> 01:32:05.626
...especially this late in the game.

918
01:32:05.894 --> 01:32:08.260
My father talked about things
he never did...

919
01:32:08.430 --> 01:32:11.797
...and I'm sure he did a lot of things
that he never talked about.

920
01:32:11.967 --> 01:32:13.992
I'm just trying to reconcile the two.

921
01:32:17.439 --> 01:32:20.533
The first thing
you have to understand...

922
01:32:20.709 --> 01:32:23.678
...is that your father
never meant to end up here.

923
01:32:24.246 --> 01:32:25.611
Yet he did.

924
01:32:25.814 --> 01:32:27.509
Twice.

925
01:32:28.050 --> 01:32:30.541
The first time, he was early.

926
01:32:30.719 --> 01:32:32.949
The second time, he was late.

927
01:32:35.057 --> 01:32:38.959
<i>Those days, your father was working</i>
<i>for himself.</i>

928
01:32:39.128 --> 01:32:42.063
<i>If there's one thing you could</i>
<i>say about Edward Bloom...</i>

929
01:32:42.231 --> 01:32:47.225
<i>...it's that he was a social person,</i>
<i>and people took a liking to him.</i>

930
01:32:47.402 --> 01:32:50.633
<i>One night, he was returning</i>
<i>from three weeks on the road...</i>

931
01:32:50.806 --> 01:32:54.503
<i>...when he hit a thunderstorm</i>
<i>unlike any in his life.</i>

932
01:34:42.818 --> 01:34:45.343
<i>Fate has a way of circling back</i>
<i>on a man...</i>

933
01:34:45.520 --> 01:34:47.613
<i>...and taking him by surprise.</i>

934
01:34:52.361 --> 01:34:56.024
<i>A man sees things differently</i>
<i>at different times in his life.</i>

935
01:34:56.198 --> 01:35:00.134
<i>This town didn't seem the same</i>
<i>now that he was older.</i>

936
01:35:01.336 --> 01:35:04.863
<i>A new road had brought</i>
<i>the outside world to Spectre...</i>

937
01:35:05.040 --> 01:35:08.168
<i>...and with it, banks, liens and debt.</i>

938
01:35:08.343 --> 01:35:13.178
<i>Almost everywhere you looked,</i>
<i>people were bankrupt.</i>

939
01:35:14.449 --> 01:35:17.816
The auction today
is for the town of Spectre.

940
01:35:17.986 --> 01:35:20.716
-The opening bid will be $10,000.
-<i>So Edward Bloom...</i>

941
01:35:20.889 --> 01:35:23.517
<i>-...decided to buy the town.</i>
-50,000.

942
01:35:27.696 --> 01:35:30.893
-I couldn't believe my eyes...
-<i>He was never a wealthy man...</i>

943
01:35:31.066 --> 01:35:36.231
<i>...but he had made other men rich,</i>
<i>and now he asked for their favors.</i>

944
01:35:38.140 --> 01:35:41.166
-I'm trying to save...
-<i>Most of them hadn't seen Spectre.</i>

945
01:35:41.343 --> 01:35:44.574
<i>They only had Edward's words</i>
<i>to describe it.</i>

946
01:35:44.746 --> 01:35:48.045
<i>That's all he needed.</i>
<i>He sold them on the dream.</i>

947
01:35:48.450 --> 01:35:50.941
<i>So first he bought the farms.</i>

948
01:35:51.119 --> 01:35:55.021
<i>Then he bought the houses.</i>
<i>And then he bought the stores.</i>

949
01:35:55.624 --> 01:36:00.721
<i>Whatever he bought, the people were</i>
<i>not asked to leave or pay rent.</i>

950
01:36:00.896 --> 01:36:03.797
<i>They were just asked to keep</i>
<i>doing as they were doing.</i>

951
01:36:04.166 --> 01:36:08.193
<i>In that way, he could make sure</i>
<i>the town would never die.</i>

952
01:36:10.639 --> 01:36:15.906
<i>Within six months, his trust had</i>
<i>purchased the entire town.</i>

953
01:36:16.078 --> 01:36:18.740
<i>With one exception.</i>

954
01:36:42.971 --> 01:36:46.031
You must be Edward Bloom.

955
01:36:46.208 --> 01:36:47.505
How do you know?

956
01:36:48.110 --> 01:36:50.806
No one would come out here
unless they had business.

957
01:36:51.013 --> 01:36:54.005
And no one would have business
with me except for you.

958
01:36:54.182 --> 01:36:56.548
You're buying the town.

959
01:36:57.052 --> 01:37:01.079
Apparently I overlooked this one
piece of it, and I'd like to remedy that.

960
01:37:01.690 --> 01:37:06.593
In order for the town to be preserved,
the trust must own it, in its entirety.

961
01:37:06.762 --> 01:37:08.127
And so I've heard.

962
01:37:08.296 --> 01:37:11.754
I'll offer you more than it's worth.
And you won't have to move.

963
01:37:11.933 --> 01:37:15.369
Nothing will change except the name
on the deed, you have my word.

964
01:37:15.537 --> 01:37:18.199
Now, let me get this straight.

965
01:37:18.373 --> 01:37:21.035
You buy the swamp from me,
but I'll stay in it?

966
01:37:21.209 --> 01:37:23.871
You'll own the house,
but it'll still be mine?

967
01:37:24.046 --> 01:37:28.039
I'll be here, and you'll come and go as
you please to one place or another.

968
01:37:28.216 --> 01:37:30.184
Do I have that right?

969
01:37:30.352 --> 01:37:32.946
In so many words, yes.

970
01:37:33.822 --> 01:37:35.790
Yeah, then I don't think so,
Mr. Bloom.

971
01:37:36.458 --> 01:37:39.484
If nothing's gonna change,
I'd just as soon it not change...

972
01:37:39.661 --> 01:37:42.630
...in the way things haven't been
changing all this time.

973
01:37:43.865 --> 01:37:48.632
It's not like you'll lose anything.
You can ask anyone in town.

974
01:37:50.038 --> 01:37:52.529
Why are you buying this land,
Mr. Bloom?

975
01:37:52.707 --> 01:37:54.265
Some sort of midlife crisis?

976
01:37:55.043 --> 01:37:57.807
Instead of buying a convertible,
you buy a town?

977
01:37:58.280 --> 01:38:00.441
Helping people makes me happy.

978
01:38:01.149 --> 01:38:04.312
I'm not convinced
you should be happy.

979
01:38:04.486 --> 01:38:08.445
-I'm sorry. Have I offended you?
-No.

980
01:38:08.623 --> 01:38:10.523
You did exactly what you promised.

981
01:38:11.159 --> 01:38:15.789
You came back.
I was just expecting you sooner.

982
01:38:23.338 --> 01:38:24.930
You're Beamen's daughter.

983
01:38:26.741 --> 01:38:29.471
Your name's different.
Did you get married?

984
01:38:30.212 --> 01:38:35.275
I was 18. He was 28.
Turns out that was a big difference.

985
01:38:44.893 --> 01:38:48.158
I won't be selling you this house,
Mr. Bloom.

986
01:38:49.064 --> 01:38:50.793
I see.

987
01:38:51.800 --> 01:38:54.200
Well, thank you for your time.

988
01:39:05.447 --> 01:39:08.575
-It's stuck.
-Yeah.

989
01:39:18.860 --> 01:39:21.920
-Oh, I'm so sorry! I...
-It's okay. Just leave it.

990
01:39:22.097 --> 01:39:24.588
-No, I'll...
-Please! Just go.

991
01:39:26.134 --> 01:39:28.694
-But...
-Go.

992
01:39:31.006 --> 01:39:35.773
<i>Most men in that situation would</i>
<i>accept their failure and move on.</i>

993
01:39:35.944 --> 01:39:38.013
<i>But Edward was not like most men.</i>

994
01:39:38.013 --> 01:39:39.002
<i>But Edward was not like most men.</i>

995
01:39:48.356 --> 01:39:49.789
Hold!

996
01:39:53.228 --> 01:39:56.061
They both had completely
different characters but...

997
01:39:56.231 --> 01:39:58.927
...well, just one pair of legs.

998
01:40:09.277 --> 01:40:12.838
<i>As the months passed, he found</i>
<i>more and more things to fix...</i>

999
01:40:13.014 --> 01:40:16.916
<i>...until the shack no longer</i>
<i>resembled itself.</i>

1000
01:40:33.134 --> 01:40:37.161
Of course, the best part
was coming up with the new material.

1001
01:40:37.939 --> 01:40:40.407
By the time the twins and I
had got to Havana...

1002
01:40:40.575 --> 01:40:46.172
...we'd worked out a new routine,
using only a ukulele and a harmonica.

1003
01:41:05.467 --> 01:41:09.028
-Well...
-You could leave it there.

1004
01:41:26.554 --> 01:41:28.146
No.

1005
01:41:38.233 --> 01:41:40.167
Now, don't.

1006
01:41:40.335 --> 01:41:45.500
Don't be embarrassed. I should never
have let you think that...

1007
01:41:46.341 --> 01:41:49.139
I'm in love with my wife.

1008
01:41:50.145 --> 01:41:52.136
Yeah, I know.

1009
01:41:52.314 --> 01:41:55.442
And from the first day I saw her
until the day I die...

1010
01:41:55.617 --> 01:41:57.676
...she's the only one.

1011
01:41:58.420 --> 01:42:00.354
Lucky girl.

1012
01:42:01.389 --> 01:42:04.324
I'm sorry, Jenny. I am.

1013
01:42:12.233 --> 01:42:14.531
Wait! Edward.

1014
01:42:56.111 --> 01:42:59.012
<i>One day, Edward Bloom left...</i>

1015
01:42:59.180 --> 01:43:03.014
<i>...and never returned</i>
<i>to the town he'd saved.</i>

1016
01:43:15.563 --> 01:43:17.394
<i>As for the girl...</i>

1017
01:43:17.565 --> 01:43:20.261
<i>...the common belief</i>
<i>was that she'd become a witch...</i>

1018
01:43:20.435 --> 01:43:22.835
<i>...and crazy at that.</i>

1019
01:43:24.272 --> 01:43:27.730
<i>She became something</i>
<i>of a legend herself.</i>

1020
01:43:29.010 --> 01:43:33.413
<i>And the story ended where it began.</i>

1021
01:43:34.549 --> 01:43:37.450
Logically, you couldn't be
the witch, because she was...

1022
01:43:37.619 --> 01:43:38.916
...old when he was young.

1023
01:43:39.220 --> 01:43:42.348
Well, it's logical if you think
like your father.

1024
01:43:43.324 --> 01:43:47.192
See, to him there's only
two women:

1025
01:43:47.362 --> 01:43:48.954
Your mother...

1026
01:43:49.130 --> 01:43:50.495
...and everyone else.

1027
01:43:50.765 --> 01:43:52.665
And one day...

1028
01:43:52.834 --> 01:43:57.294
...I realized I was in love with a man
who could never love me back.

1029
01:43:58.339 --> 01:44:00.500
I was living in a fairy tale.

1030
01:44:01.643 --> 01:44:03.975
I'm not sure I should've told you
any of this.

1031
01:44:04.145 --> 01:44:06.670
No, no, no, I wanted to know. I'm...

1032
01:44:06.848 --> 01:44:08.315
I'm glad I know.

1033
01:44:09.017 --> 01:44:12.350
I wanted to be as important to him
as you were.

1034
01:44:12.854 --> 01:44:14.651
And I'm...

1035
01:44:15.123 --> 01:44:16.522
I was never gonna be.

1036
01:44:19.494 --> 01:44:21.655
I was make-believe.

1037
01:44:23.131 --> 01:44:27.500
And his other life, you...

1038
01:44:28.336 --> 01:44:29.894
You were real.

1039
01:44:54.195 --> 01:44:55.787
Mom?

1040
01:44:56.831 --> 01:44:58.662
Josephine?

1041
01:45:00.502 --> 01:45:02.367
Josephine?

1042
01:45:16.017 --> 01:45:18.076
Will!

1043
01:45:19.220 --> 01:45:20.448
What happened?

1044
01:45:20.622 --> 01:45:22.385
Your father had a stroke.

1045
01:45:22.557 --> 01:45:25.685
He's upstairs with your mom
and Dr. Bennett.

1046
01:45:25.860 --> 01:45:27.191
Is he gonna be okay?

1047
01:45:35.136 --> 01:45:37.229
I don't suppose that I could...

1048
01:45:37.405 --> 01:45:39.896
...stay here with him.
I mean, in case...

1049
01:45:40.742 --> 01:45:43.677
In case he wakes up,
I really ought to be here.

1050
01:45:43.845 --> 01:45:45.107
I'll stay.

1051
01:45:46.147 --> 01:45:49.173
Why don't you go home with
Josephine. I'll stay tonight.

1052
01:45:51.019 --> 01:45:53.988
-Is that okay?
-Fine.

1053
01:45:56.891 --> 01:45:59.792
Will, you'll call if there's any?

1054
01:45:59.961 --> 01:46:03.089
I will. I will. I'll call.

1055
01:46:04.666 --> 01:46:08.659
-Do you want some time with Dad?
-Yes. Thank you.

1056
01:47:12.767 --> 01:47:16.294
Glad to see you're not trying
to have a heartfelt talk.

1057
01:47:19.474 --> 01:47:22.068
One of my greatest
annoyances is when...

1058
01:47:22.243 --> 01:47:25.178
...people try to talk to those
who can't hear them.

1059
01:47:25.346 --> 01:47:27.473
Well, we have an advantage.

1060
01:47:27.649 --> 01:47:30.083
My father and I never talk.

1061
01:47:36.624 --> 01:47:39.684
Your father ever tell you
about the day you were born?

1062
01:47:39.861 --> 01:47:43.297
Yeah, a thousand times.
He caught an uncatchable fish.

1063
01:47:43.464 --> 01:47:47.423
Not that. The real story.
He ever tell you that?

1064
01:47:47.602 --> 01:47:49.433
No.

1065
01:47:49.604 --> 01:47:53.062
Well, your mother came in about
3 in the afternoon.

1066
01:47:53.241 --> 01:47:57.701
Her neighbor drove her, on account of
your father was away on business...

1067
01:47:57.879 --> 01:47:59.540
...in Wichita.

1068
01:47:59.714 --> 01:48:04.048
You were born a week early,
but there were no complications.

1069
01:48:04.218 --> 01:48:09.884
It was a perfect delivery.
Your father was sorry not to be there.

1070
01:48:10.425 --> 01:48:15.158
But it wasn't the custom then for men
to be in the room for deliveries...

1071
01:48:15.329 --> 01:48:19.595
...so I can't see how it would've been
much different had he been there.

1072
01:48:19.767 --> 01:48:22.531
And that's the real story
of how you were born.

1073
01:48:23.171 --> 01:48:25.605
Not very exciting, is it?

1074
01:48:25.773 --> 01:48:29.641
And I suppose if I had to choose
between the true version...

1075
01:48:29.811 --> 01:48:34.339
...and an elaborate one involving
a fish and a wedding ring...

1076
01:48:34.515 --> 01:48:36.483
...I might choose the fancy version.

1077
01:48:37.185 --> 01:48:39.153
But, then that's just me.

1078
01:48:40.221 --> 01:48:41.745
I kind of liked your version.

1079
01:49:04.312 --> 01:49:05.904
Dad?

1080
01:49:08.049 --> 01:49:11.610
Hey, Dad? Hey, you want me
to get the nurse?

1081
01:49:13.488 --> 01:49:16.548
What do you want? What can I do?
What can I get?

1082
01:49:16.724 --> 01:49:18.783
You want some water?

1083
01:49:19.861 --> 01:49:21.351
You want a bit of water?

1084
01:49:24.265 --> 01:49:26.961
The river.

1085
01:49:28.503 --> 01:49:30.368
The river?

1086
01:49:30.838 --> 01:49:35.036
Tell me how it happens.

1087
01:49:36.911 --> 01:49:38.845
How what happens?

1088
01:49:39.881 --> 01:49:42.315
How I go.

1089
01:49:46.687 --> 01:49:49.155
You mean what you saw in The Eye?

1090
01:50:03.171 --> 01:50:06.504
I don't know that story, Dad.
You never told me that one.

1091
01:50:09.377 --> 01:50:12.437
Okay. Hey, okay, I'll try.

1092
01:50:12.613 --> 01:50:15.241
I need your help.
Tell me how it starts.

1093
01:50:17.485 --> 01:50:21.148
Like this.

1094
01:50:21.322 --> 01:50:23.347
Okay. Okay.

1095
01:50:25.993 --> 01:50:29.451
Okay, so it's in the morning...

1096
01:50:29.630 --> 01:50:33.726
...and you and I are in the hospital,
and I've fallen asleep. And I wake up...

1097
01:50:34.202 --> 01:50:37.603
<i>...and I see you,</i>
<i>and somehow you're better.</i>

1098
01:50:37.772 --> 01:50:38.864
Dad?

1099
01:50:39.040 --> 01:50:41.065
<i>You're different.</i>

1100
01:50:41.976 --> 01:50:44.968
-Dad.
-Let's get out of here.

1101
01:50:45.146 --> 01:50:46.408
<i>Then I say:</i>

1102
01:50:46.581 --> 01:50:48.014
Dad, you're in no condition...

1103
01:50:48.182 --> 01:50:50.582
Get that wheelchair.

1104
01:50:53.020 --> 01:50:54.920
Hurry up! We haven't much time!

1105
01:50:55.089 --> 01:50:57.557
Once we get off this floor,
we're in the clear.

1106
01:50:57.725 --> 01:50:59.420
And we get in the wheelchair...

1107
01:50:59.594 --> 01:51:02.961
-Faster!
-<i>...like we're escaping the hospital.</i>

1108
01:51:03.130 --> 01:51:04.927
What are you doing?

1109
01:51:05.132 --> 01:51:07.464
<i>We pass Dr. Bennett,</i>
<i>who tries to slow us down.</i>

1110
01:51:07.635 --> 01:51:10.604
-Stop them!
-<i>We're flying down the hall.</i>

1111
01:51:10.771 --> 01:51:12.705
Orderly after orderly is chasing us.

1112
01:51:12.874 --> 01:51:14.933
<i>Mom and Josephine</i>
<i>are at the end.</i>

1113
01:51:15.109 --> 01:51:17.873
No time to explain! Stall them!

1114
01:51:23.551 --> 01:51:25.576
We come flying out
over the curb...

1115
01:51:25.753 --> 01:51:30.122
<i>...and your old red Charger is there.</i>
<i>But it's new. Brand-new.</i>

1116
01:51:30.291 --> 01:51:32.623
And I pick you up...

1117
01:51:32.793 --> 01:51:36.422
...and somehow you hardly weigh
anything. I can't explain it.

1118
01:51:40.701 --> 01:51:43.431
Leave it! We don't need it!

1119
01:51:45.506 --> 01:51:47.838
Water. I need water.

1120
01:51:51.145 --> 01:51:53.170
-Where are we going?
-The river.

1121
01:52:00.154 --> 01:52:02.645
And we have to take Glenville
to avoid traffic...

1122
01:52:02.823 --> 01:52:05.587
...because the damn church
people drive too slow.

1123
01:52:46.367 --> 01:52:51.634
And as we get close to the river...

1124
01:53:00.281 --> 01:53:02.476
He's here!

1125
01:53:02.783 --> 01:53:05.650
<i>...we see that everybody</i>
<i>is already there.</i>

1126
01:53:22.069 --> 01:53:23.661
And I mean...

1127
01:53:24.939 --> 01:53:26.201
...everyone.

1128
01:53:38.719 --> 01:53:41.415
It's unbelievable.

1129
01:53:41.956 --> 01:53:46.086
The story of my life.

1130
01:53:54.668 --> 01:53:57.603
The strange thing is, there's
not a sad face to be found.

1131
01:53:57.772 --> 01:54:00.332
Everyone is just so glad
to see you...

1132
01:54:01.442 --> 01:54:03.740
...and send you off right.

1133
01:54:03.911 --> 01:54:09.543
Goodbye, everybody!
Farewell! Adieu!

1134
01:54:20.895 --> 01:54:23.489
My girl in the river.

1135
01:55:35.035 --> 01:55:37.868
You become what you always were.

1136
01:55:39.507 --> 01:55:41.873
A very big fish.

1137
01:55:46.981 --> 01:55:49.313
And that's how it happens.

1138
01:55:50.885 --> 01:55:52.682
Yeah.

1139
01:55:55.156 --> 01:55:57.716
Exactly.

1140
01:56:39.800 --> 01:56:41.199
Mom?

1141
01:58:08.422 --> 01:58:11.653
The Lord is my shepherd,
I shall not be in want.

1142
01:58:11.825 --> 01:58:15.352
He makes me to lie down
in green pastures.

1143
01:58:15.529 --> 01:58:18.498
He leads me beside the quiet waters.

1144
01:58:18.666 --> 01:58:20.634
He restores my soul.

1145
01:58:20.801 --> 01:58:24.794
He guides me in the paths of
righteousness for his name's sake.

1146
01:58:25.439 --> 01:58:28.670
Yea, though I walk through
the valley of the shadow of death...

1147
01:58:28.842 --> 01:58:31.470
...I will fear no evil,
for you are with me.

1148
01:58:31.645 --> 01:58:35.979
Surely goodness and love will
follow me all the days of my life.

1149
01:59:22.963 --> 01:59:27.400
<i>Have you ever heard a joke so many</i>
<i>times you've forgotten why it's funny?</i>

1150
01:59:27.568 --> 01:59:31.664
<i>And then you hear it again</i>
<i>and suddenly it's new.</i>

1151
01:59:31.839 --> 01:59:35.002
<i>You remember why you loved it</i>
<i>in the first place.</i>

1152
01:59:36.844 --> 01:59:40.041
So he said he'll fight the giant
who was 15 feet tall!

1153
01:59:40.214 --> 01:59:43.650
-No way!
-Dad! That's right, isn't it?

1154
01:59:43.817 --> 01:59:45.148
Pretty much.

1155
01:59:45.319 --> 01:59:47.549
See? So he was a giant.

1156
01:59:47.721 --> 01:59:50.189
<i>That was my father's final joke,</i>
<i>I guess.</i>

1157
01:59:50.357 --> 01:59:56.296
<i>A man tells his stories so many times</i>
<i>that he becomes the stories.</i>

1158
01:59:57.731 --> 02:00:00.063
<i>They live on after him.</i>

1159
02:00:02.336 --> 02:00:05.703
<i>And in that way,</i>
<i>he becomes immortal.</i>
